====================================================================================
CLAIM 1 -- does the zero-norm convention change the identified subspace?
   Design: a law with a genuine ATOM AT ZERO (20% of draws set to 0),
   plus a direction h0 that X never loads on, so ker A is nontrivial and
   its dimension is known in advance.
====================================================================================
   P(X=0) = 0.00 |  th=0.0: rank 11, rel 8.1e-04  th=0.5: rank 11, rel 7.7e-04  th=1.0: rank 11, rel 9.1e-04  th=2.0: rank 11, rel 1.8e-03
   P(X=0) = 0.20 |  th=0.0: rank 11, rel 7.9e-04  th=0.5: rank 11, rel 9.9e-04  th=1.0: rank 11, rel 1.5e-03  th=2.0: rank 11, rel 3.7e-03
   P(X=0) = 0.50 |  th=0.0: rank 11, rel 8.4e-04  th=0.5: rank 11, rel 8.1e-04  th=1.0: rank 11, rel 7.9e-04  th=2.0: rank 11, rel 2.0e-03
   d = 12, and the last coordinate is never loaded, so the population
   rank is 11 at every theta if the convention leaves ker unchanged.

   VERDICT: the rank is the same at every theta and every atom size, and
   the normal equation stays exact.  On {X = 0} the form <h,X>^2 is zero
   regardless, so the indicator cannot change which h annihilate it:
   ker A_theta = {h : <h,X> = 0 a.s.}, UNCHANGED.  The amendment recorded
   in EMPIRICAL-FINDINGS.md section 2 was too strong and is corrected.

